1
00:00:03,639 --> 00:00:08,639
My sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, was more than twenty years older than I,

2
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:12,960
and had established a great reputation with herself and the neighbours because she had

3
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:19,200
brought me up “by hand.” Having at that time to find out for myself what the expression meant,

4
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,799
and knowing her to have a hard and heavy hand, and to be much in the habit of laying it upon

5
00:00:23,799 --> 00:00:31,000
her husband as well as upon me, I supposed that Joe Gargery and I were both brought up by hand.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,120
She was not a good-looking woman, my sister; and I had a general impression that she must

7
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:41,120
have made Joe Gargery marry her by hand. Joe was a fair man, with curls of flaxen hair on

8
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:46,679
each side of his smooth face, and with eyes of such a very undecided blue that they seemed to

9
00:00:46,679 --> 00:00:52,679
have somehow got mixed with their own whites. He was a mild, good-natured, sweet-tempered,

10
00:00:52,679 --> 00:01:00,480
easy-going, foolish, dear fellow,—a sort of Hercules in strength, and also in weakness.

11
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:06,759
My sister, Mrs. Joe, with black hair and eyes, had such a prevailing redness of skin that I

12
00:01:06,759 --> 00:01:10,759
sometimes used to wonder whether it was possible she washed herself with a nutmeg-grater instead

13
00:01:10,759 --> 00:01:17,440
of soap. She was tall and bony, and almost always wore a coarse apron, fastened over her

14
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:23,399
figure behind with two loops, and having a square impregnable bib in front, that was stuck full of

15
00:01:23,399 --> 00:01:30,120
pins and needles. She made it a powerful merit in herself, and a strong reproach against Joe,

16
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:36,480
that she wore this apron so much. Though I really see no reason why she should have worn it at all;

17
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:43,120
or why, if she did wear it at all, she should not have taken it off, every day of her life.

18
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:47,720
Joe’s forge adjoined our house, which was a wooden house, as many of the dwellings

19
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:54,120
in our country were,—most of them, at that time. When I ran home from the churchyard,

20
00:01:54,120 --> 00:02:01,799
the forge was shut up, and Joe was sitting alone in the kitchen. Joe and I being fellow-sufferers,

21
00:02:01,799 --> 00:02:08,279
and having confidences as such, Joe imparted a confidence to me, the moment I raised the latch

22
00:02:08,279 --> 00:02:14,759
of the door and peeped in at him opposite to it, sitting in the chimney corner.

23
00:02:14,759 --> 00:02:17,039
“Mrs. Joe has been out a dozen times,

24
00:02:17,039 --> 00:02:22,159
looking for you, Pip. And she’s out now, making it a baker’s dozen.”

25
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:23,440
“Is she?”

26
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:29,279
“Yes, Pip,” said Joe; “and what’s worse, she’s got Tickler with her.”

27
00:02:29,279 --> 00:02:34,320
At this dismal intelligence, I twisted the only button on my waistcoat round and round,

28
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:40,159
and looked in great depression at the fire. Tickler was a wax-ended piece of cane,

29
00:02:40,159 --> 00:02:44,120
worn smooth by collision with my tickled frame.

30
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:48,960
“She sot down,” said Joe, “and she got up, and she made a grab at Tickler,

31
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:53,879
and she Ram-paged out. That’s what she did,” said Joe, slowly clearing the fire

32
00:02:53,879 --> 00:02:59,720
between the lower bars with the poker, and looking at it; “she Ram-paged out, Pip.”

33
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:05,360
“Has she been gone long, Joe?” I always treated him as a larger species of child,

34
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:08,240
and as no more than my equal.

35
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:11,679
“Well,” said Joe, glancing up at the Dutch clock,

36
00:03:11,679 --> 00:03:15,559
“she’s been on the Ram-page, this last spell, about five minutes,

37
00:03:15,559 --> 00:03:22,120
Pip. She’s a-coming! Get behind the door, old chap, and have the jack-towel betwixt you.”

38
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:29,080
I took the advice. My sister, Mrs. Joe, throwing the door wide open, and finding an obstruction

39
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:36,080
behind it, immediately divined the cause, and applied Tickler to its further investigation.

40
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:43,320
She concluded by throwing me—I often served as a connubial missile—at Joe, who, glad to get hold

41
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:50,200
of me on any terms, passed me on into the chimney and quietly fenced me up there with his great leg.

42
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:55,399
“Where have you been, you young monkey?” said Mrs. Joe, stamping her foot. “Tell

43
00:03:55,399 --> 00:03:59,559
me directly what you’ve been doing to wear me away with fret and fright and worrit,

44
00:03:59,559 --> 00:04:05,399
or I’d have you out of that corner if you was fifty Pips, and he was five hundred Gargerys.”

45
00:04:05,399 --> 00:04:11,000
“I have only been to the churchyard,” said I, from my stool, crying and rubbing myself.

46
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,240
“Churchyard!” repeated my sister. “If it warn’t for me you’d have been to

47
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:19,960
the churchyard long ago, and stayed there. Who brought you up by hand?”

48
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:22,679
“You did,” said I.

49
00:04:22,679 --> 00:04:27,240
“And why did I do it, I should like to know?” exclaimed my sister.

50
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,519
I whimpered, “I don’t know.”

51
00:04:30,519 --> 00:04:35,279
“I don’t!” said my sister. “I’d never do it again! I know that. I may truly

52
00:04:35,279 --> 00:04:39,960
say I’ve never had this apron of mine off since born you were. It’s bad enough

53
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:45,600
to be a blacksmith’s wife (and him a Gargery) without being your mother.”

54
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:49,919
My thoughts strayed from that question as I looked disconsolately at the fire.

55
00:04:50,759 --> 00:04:55,799
For the fugitive out on the marshes with the ironed leg, the mysterious young man,

56
00:04:55,799 --> 00:05:00,600
the file, the food, and the dreadful pledge I was under to commit a larceny

57
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:06,320
on those sheltering premises, rose before me in the avenging coals.

58
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:11,159
“Hah!” said Mrs. Joe, restoring Tickler to his station. “Churchyard,

59
00:05:11,159 --> 00:05:16,639
indeed! You may well say churchyard, you two.” One of us, by the by,

60
00:05:16,639 --> 00:05:22,440
had not said it at all. “You’ll drive me to the churchyard betwixt you, one of these days,

61
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:31,120
and O, a pr-r-recious pair you’d be without me!” As she applied herself to set the tea-things,

62
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:36,600
Joe peeped down at me over his leg, as if he were mentally casting me and himself up,

63
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:41,799
and calculating what kind of pair we practically should make, under the grievous circumstances

64
00:05:41,799 --> 00:05:49,120
foreshadowed. After that, he sat feeling his right-side flaxen curls and whisker,

65
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:56,319
and following Mrs. Joe about with his blue eyes, as his manner always was at squally times.

66
00:05:56,319 --> 00:06:02,000
My sister had a trenchant way of cutting our bread and butter for us, that never varied. First,

67
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,880
with her left hand she jammed the loaf hard and fast against her bib,—where it sometimes got a

68
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:12,720
pin into it, and sometimes a needle, which we afterwards got into our mouths. Then she took

69
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:18,160
some butter (not too much) on a knife and spread it on the loaf, in an apothecary kind of way,

70
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:23,759
as if she were making a plaster,—using both sides of the knife with a slapping dexterity,

71
00:06:23,759 --> 00:06:29,039
and trimming and moulding the butter off round the crust. Then, she gave the knife a final smart

72
00:06:29,039 --> 00:06:35,000
wipe on the edge of the plaster, and then sawed a very thick round off the loaf: which she finally,

73
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:42,119
before separating from the loaf, hewed into two halves, of which Joe got one, and I the other.

74
00:06:42,119 --> 00:06:48,480
On the present occasion, though I was hungry, I dared not eat my slice. I felt that I must have

75
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:52,279
something in reserve for my dreadful acquaintance, and his ally the still

76
00:06:52,279 --> 00:06:57,880
more dreadful young man. I knew Mrs. Joe’s housekeeping to be of the strictest kind,

77
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:03,559
and that my larcenous researches might find nothing available in the safe. Therefore I

78
00:07:03,559 --> 00:07:07,720
resolved to put my hunk of bread and butter down the leg of my trousers.

79
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:11,599
The effort of resolution necessary to the achievement of this purpose I found to be

80
00:07:11,599 --> 00:07:17,079
quite awful. It was as if I had to make up my mind to leap from the top of a high house,

81
00:07:17,079 --> 00:07:22,519
or plunge into a great depth of water. And it was made the more difficult by the unconscious

82
00:07:22,519 --> 00:07:28,640
Joe. In our already-mentioned freemasonry as fellow-sufferers, and in his good-natured

83
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:34,480
companionship with me, it was our evening habit to compare the way we bit through our slices,

84
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:40,000
by silently holding them up to each other’s admiration now and then,—which stimulated us

85
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,720
to new exertions. To-night, Joe several times invited me, by the display of his

86
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:53,279
fast diminishing slice, to enter upon our usual friendly competition; but he found me, each time,

87
00:07:53,279 --> 00:07:59,839
with my yellow mug of tea on one knee, and my untouched bread and butter on the other. At last,

88
00:07:59,839 --> 00:08:04,799
I desperately considered that the thing I contemplated must be done, and that it had

89
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:10,559
best be done in the least improbable manner consistent with the circumstances. I took

90
00:08:10,559 --> 00:08:16,519
advantage of a moment when Joe had just looked at me, and got my bread and butter down my leg.

91
00:08:16,519 --> 00:08:21,000
Joe was evidently made uncomfortable by what he supposed to be my loss of appetite,

92
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:27,200
and took a thoughtful bite out of his slice, which he didn’t seem to enjoy. He turned it about in his

93
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:33,079
mouth much longer than usual, pondering over it a good deal, and after all gulped it down like

94
00:08:33,079 --> 00:08:39,039
a pill. He was about to take another bite, and had just got his head on one side for a

95
00:08:39,039 --> 00:08:47,079
good purchase on it, when his eye fell on me, and he saw that my bread and butter was gone.

96
00:08:47,079 --> 00:08:51,119
The wonder and consternation with which Joe stopped on the threshold of his bite

97
00:08:51,119 --> 00:08:57,319
and stared at me, were too evident to escape my sister’s observation.

98
00:08:57,319 --> 00:09:02,359
“What’s the matter now?” said she, smartly, as she put down her cup.

99
00:09:02,359 --> 00:09:08,599
“I say, you know!” muttered Joe, shaking his head at me in very serious remonstrance. “Pip,

100
00:09:08,599 --> 00:09:15,720
old chap! You’ll do yourself a mischief. It’ll stick somewhere. You can’t have chawed it, Pip.”

101
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,640
“What’s the matter now?” repeated my sister, more sharply than before.

102
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:24,640
“If you can cough any trifle on it up, Pip, I’d recommend you to do it,” said Joe,

103
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:30,039
all aghast. “Manners is manners, but still your elth’s your elth.”

104
00:09:30,039 --> 00:09:33,759
By this time, my sister was quite desperate, so she pounced on Joe,

105
00:09:33,759 --> 00:09:37,160
and, taking him by the two whiskers, knocked his head for a little while

106
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:43,039
against the wall behind him, while I sat in the corner, looking guiltily on.

107
00:09:43,039 --> 00:09:45,599
“Now, perhaps you’ll mention what’s the matter,” said my sister,

108
00:09:45,599 --> 00:09:50,000
out of breath, “you staring great stuck pig.”

109
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:56,559
Joe looked at her in a helpless way, then took a helpless bite, and looked at me again.

110
00:09:56,559 --> 00:10:01,359
“You know, Pip,” said Joe, solemnly, with his last bite in his cheek,

111
00:10:01,359 --> 00:10:08,359
and speaking in a confidential voice, as if we two were quite alone, “you and me is always friends,

112
00:10:08,359 --> 00:10:14,519
and I’d be the last to tell upon you, any time. But such a—” he moved his chair and looked about

113
00:10:14,519 --> 00:10:19,519
the floor between us, and then again at me—“such a most oncommon Bolt as that!”

114
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:24,279
“Been bolting his food, has he?” cried my sister.

115
00:10:24,279 --> 00:10:28,759
“You know, old chap,” said Joe, looking at me, and not at Mrs. Joe, with his bite still

116
00:10:28,759 --> 00:10:34,960
in his cheek, “I Bolted, myself, when I was your age—frequent—and as a boy I’ve been among a many

117
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:41,279
Bolters; but I never see your Bolting equal yet, Pip, and it’s a mercy you ain’t Bolted dead.”

118
00:10:41,279 --> 00:10:45,599
My sister made a dive at me, and fished me up by the hair, saying nothing more than the

119
00:10:45,599 --> 00:10:52,400
awful words, “You come along and be dosed.” Some medical beast had revived Tar-water in

120
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:57,559
those days as a fine medicine, and Mrs. Joe always kept a supply of it in the cupboard;

121
00:10:57,559 --> 00:11:03,319
having a belief in its virtues correspondent to its nastiness. At the best of times, so much of

122
00:11:03,319 --> 00:11:08,680
this elixir was administered to me as a choice restorative, that I was conscious of going about,

123
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:15,039
smelling like a new fence. On this particular evening the urgency of my case demanded a pint

124
00:11:15,039 --> 00:11:21,119
of this mixture, which was poured down my throat, for my greater comfort, while Mrs. Joe held my

125
00:11:21,119 --> 00:11:28,599
head under her arm, as a boot would be held in a bootjack. Joe got off with half a pint; but was

126
00:11:28,599 --> 00:11:34,440
made to swallow that (much to his disturbance, as he sat slowly munching and meditating before the

127
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:41,720
fire), “because he had had a turn.” Judging from myself, I should say he certainly had

128
00:11:41,720 --> 00:11:48,079
a turn afterwards, if he had had none before. Conscience is a dreadful thing when it accuses

129
00:11:48,079 --> 00:11:54,880
man or boy; but when, in the case of a boy, that secret burden co-operates with another

130
00:11:54,880 --> 00:12:02,759
secret burden down the leg of his trousers, it is (as I can testify) a great punishment. The guilty

131
00:12:02,759 --> 00:12:08,680
knowledge that I was going to rob Mrs. Joe—I never thought I was going to rob Joe, for I never

132
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:14,359
thought of any of the housekeeping property as his—united to the necessity of always keeping one

133
00:12:14,359 --> 00:12:19,440
hand on my bread and butter as I sat, or when I was ordered about the kitchen on any small errand,

134
00:12:20,319 --> 00:12:27,880
almost drove me out of my mind. Then, as the marsh winds made the fire glow and flare, I

135
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:34,680
thought I heard the voice outside, of the man with the iron on his leg who had sworn me to secrecy,

136
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:41,200
declaring that he couldn’t and wouldn’t starve until to-morrow, but must be fed now. At other

137
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:47,079
times, I thought, What if the young man who was with so much difficulty restrained from imbruing

138
00:12:47,079 --> 00:12:53,359
his hands in me should yield to a constitutional impatience, or should mistake the time, and should

139
00:12:53,359 --> 00:12:59,559
think himself accredited to my heart and liver to-night, instead of to-morrow! If ever anybody’s

140
00:12:59,559 --> 00:13:06,799
hair stood on end with terror, mine must have done so then. But, perhaps, nobody’s ever did?

141
00:13:06,799 --> 00:13:12,039
It was Christmas Eve, and I had to stir the pudding for next day, with a copper-stick,

142
00:13:12,039 --> 00:13:17,920
from seven to eight by the Dutch clock. I tried it with the load upon my leg (and that made me

143
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:22,559
think afresh of the man with the load on his leg), and found the tendency of exercise to

144
00:13:22,559 --> 00:13:28,559
bring the bread and butter out at my ankle, quite unmanageable. Happily I slipped away,

145
00:13:28,559 --> 00:13:32,720
and deposited that part of my conscience in my garret bedroom.

146
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:36,799
“Hark!” said I, when I had done my stirring, and was taking a final warm

147
00:13:36,799 --> 00:13:42,480
in the chimney corner before being sent up to bed; “was that great guns, Joe?”

148
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:46,200
“Ah!” said Joe. “There’s another conwict off.”

149
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:48,720
“What does that mean, Joe?” said I.

150
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:53,480
Mrs. Joe, who always took explanations upon herself, said, snappishly,

151
00:13:53,480 --> 00:13:58,880
“Escaped. Escaped.” Administering the definition like Tar-water.

152
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:03,640
While Mrs. Joe sat with her head bending over her needlework, I put my mouth into the forms

153
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:09,079
of saying to Joe, “What’s a convict?” Joe put his mouth into the forms of returning such

154
00:14:09,079 --> 00:14:14,920
a highly elaborate answer, that I could make out nothing of it but the single word “Pip.”

155
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:19,319
“There was a conwict off last night,” said Joe, aloud, “after sunset-gun.

156
00:14:19,839 --> 00:14:24,440
And they fired warning of him. And now it appears they’re firing warning of another.”

157
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:26,720
“Who’s firing?” said I.

158
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:30,119
“Drat that boy,” interposed my sister, frowning at me over her

159
00:14:30,119 --> 00:14:35,759
work, “what a questioner he is. Ask no questions, and you’ll be told no lies.”

160
00:14:35,759 --> 00:14:40,599
It was not very polite to herself, I thought, to imply that I should be told lies by her even

161
00:14:40,599 --> 00:14:46,279
if I did ask questions. But she never was polite unless there was company.

162
00:14:46,279 --> 00:14:51,680
At this point Joe greatly augmented my curiosity by taking the utmost pains to open his mouth very

163
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:57,160
wide, and to put it into the form of a word that looked to me like “sulks.” Therefore,

164
00:14:57,160 --> 00:15:01,160
I naturally pointed to Mrs. Joe, and put my mouth into the form of saying,

165
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:07,039
“her?” But Joe wouldn’t hear of that, at all, and again opened his mouth very wide,

166
00:15:07,039 --> 00:15:13,559
and shook the form of a most emphatic word out of it. But I could make nothing of the word.

167
00:15:13,559 --> 00:15:19,519
“Mrs. Joe,” said I, as a last resort, “I should like to know—if you wouldn’t

168
00:15:19,519 --> 00:15:23,200
much mind—where the firing comes from?”

169
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:26,240
“Lord bless the boy!” exclaimed my sister,

170
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:31,640
as if she didn’t quite mean that but rather the contrary. “From the Hulks!”

171
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:34,960
“Oh-h!” said I, looking at Joe. “Hulks!”

172
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:40,160
Joe gave a reproachful cough, as much as to say, “Well, I told you so.”

173
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:43,039
“And please, what’s Hulks?” said I.

174
00:15:43,039 --> 00:15:45,759
“That’s the way with this boy!” exclaimed my sister,

175
00:15:45,759 --> 00:15:49,440
pointing me out with her needle and thread, and shaking her head at me.

176
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:54,319
“Answer him one question, and he’ll ask you a dozen directly. Hulks are prison-ships,

177
00:15:54,319 --> 00:16:00,319
right ’cross th’ meshes.” We always used that name for marshes, in our country.

178
00:16:00,319 --> 00:16:05,359
“I wonder who’s put into prison-ships, and why they’re put there?” said I, in a general way,

179
00:16:05,359 --> 00:16:10,000
and with quiet desperation. It was too much for Mrs. Joe,

180
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,920
who immediately rose. “I tell you what, young fellow,” said she,

181
00:16:14,920 --> 00:16:20,279
“I didn’t bring you up by hand to badger people’s lives out. It would be blame to me and not praise,

182
00:16:20,279 --> 00:16:25,920
if I had. People are put in the Hulks because they murder, and because they rob, and forge,

183
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:33,839
and do all sorts of bad; and they always begin by asking questions. Now, you get along to bed!”

184
00:16:33,839 --> 00:16:38,240
I was never allowed a candle to light me to bed, and, as I went upstairs in the dark,

185
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:44,200
with my head tingling,—from Mrs. Joe’s thimble having played the tambourine upon it, to accompany

186
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:49,519
her last words,—I felt fearfully sensible of the great convenience that the hulks were handy for

187
00:16:49,519 --> 00:16:57,720
me. I was clearly on my way there. I had begun by asking questions, and I was going to rob Mrs. Joe.

188
00:16:57,720 --> 00:17:02,680
Since that time, which is far enough away now, I have often thought that few people

189
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:07,640
know what secrecy there is in the young under terror. No matter how unreasonable the terror,

190
00:17:07,640 --> 00:17:14,960
so that it be terror. I was in mortal terror of the young man who wanted my heart and liver;

191
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:20,720
I was in mortal terror of my interlocutor with the iron leg; I was in mortal terror of myself,

192
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:24,240
from whom an awful promise had been extracted; I had no hope

193
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:28,960
of deliverance through my all-powerful sister, who repulsed me at every turn;

194
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:34,119
I am afraid to think of what I might have done on requirement, in the secrecy of my terror.

195
00:17:34,119 --> 00:17:39,079
If I slept at all that night, it was only to imagine myself drifting down the river on a

196
00:17:39,079 --> 00:17:46,000
strong spring-tide, to the Hulks; a ghostly pirate calling out to me through a speaking-trumpet,

197
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:51,640
as I passed the gibbet-station, that I had better come ashore and be hanged there at once,

198
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:58,880
and not put it off. I was afraid to sleep, even if I had been inclined, for I knew that at the

199
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:04,599
first faint dawn of morning I must rob the pantry. There was no doing it in the night,

200
00:18:04,599 --> 00:18:09,880
for there was no getting a light by easy friction then; to have got one I must have struck it out

201
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:15,359
of flint and steel, and have made a noise like the very pirate himself rattling his chains.

202
00:18:15,359 --> 00:18:20,640
As soon as the great black velvet pall outside my little window was shot with grey, I got up

203
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:26,799
and went downstairs; every board upon the way, and every crack in every board calling after me,

204
00:18:26,799 --> 00:18:33,480
“Stop thief!” and “Get up, Mrs. Joe!” In the pantry, which was far more abundantly supplied

205
00:18:33,480 --> 00:18:39,799
than usual, owing to the season, I was very much alarmed by a hare hanging up by the heels, whom

206
00:18:39,799 --> 00:18:46,599
I rather thought I caught, when my back was half turned, winking. I had no time for verification,

207
00:18:46,599 --> 00:18:53,400
no time for selection, no time for anything, for I had no time to spare. I stole some bread,

208
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:59,279
some rind of cheese, about half a jar of mincemeat (which I tied up in my pocket-handkerchief with my

209
00:18:59,279 --> 00:19:05,839
last night’s slice), some brandy from a stone bottle (which I decanted into a glass bottle I

210
00:19:05,839 --> 00:19:12,200
had secretly used for making that intoxicating fluid, Spanish-liquorice-water, up in my room:

211
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:16,720
diluting the stone bottle from a jug in the kitchen cupboard), a meat bone with

212
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:24,559
very little on it, and a beautiful round compact pork pie. I was nearly going away without the pie,

213
00:19:24,559 --> 00:19:30,000
but I was tempted to mount upon a shelf, to look what it was that was put away so carefully in a

214
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:35,920
covered earthenware dish in a corner, and I found it was the pie, and I took it in

215
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:42,319
the hope that it was not intended for early use, and would not be missed for some time.

216
00:19:42,319 --> 00:19:48,759
There was a door in the kitchen, communicating with the forge; I unlocked and unbolted that door,

217
00:19:48,759 --> 00:19:54,960
and got a file from among Joe’s tools. Then I put the fastenings as I had found them,

218
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:59,319
opened the door at which I had entered when I ran home last night, shut it,

219
00:19:59,319 --> 00:20:02,000
and ran for the misty marshes. Chapter 3.
